            quoted figure is very likely peak; the continuous rating, which matters on a long desk walk, is usually lower.
            YPOO's inclines are also all manual: you set one of two or three positions by hand, with the belt stopped.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ypoo-and-rhythm-fun" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>YPOO and RHYTHM FUN: The Same Machines?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The two brands are clearly linked, and some of their machines look like the same design, but we could
            not confirm that any two specific models are identical.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/rhythm-fun-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">RHYTHM FUN treadmill guide</a> noted
            that RHYTHM FUN's pads use the YPOOFIT app and that its store describes its S8 treadmill as a "YPOO treadmill".
            YPOO's side of the story adds more. The shipping guide on ypoofitness.com, checked 26 November 2026, refers to
            "Rhythm Fun" by name four times, including "the Rhythm Fun shipping facility", in a page that is otherwise
            about YPOO orders. That reads like a policy page shared between the two stores.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which models line up</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The S8 and the YPOO V8 (M4638).</strong> RHYTHM FUN describes its S8 as a handle-bar treadmill with
            a three-lever manual incline to 8%, 8 mph, 300 lb, a brushless motor under 65 dB, a Bluetooth speaker and the
            YPOOFIT app. YPOO's store describes its V8 Auto-Folding Treadmill (SKU code M4638) in almost the same words:
            "Three Lever Manual Incline", 8%, 8 mph, 300 lb, "noise below 65 db", a Bluetooth speaker and YPOOFIT. On
            Amazon, though, RHYTHM FUN's 8 mph, 8% treadmill carries model code M4438 and a 41.7 by 15-inch belt, while
            YPOO's M4638 listing gives 37 by 15 inches. They are the same class of machine, but the listings do not prove
            they are the same machine.</li>
            <li><strong>The RHYTHM FUN 16% pad and YPOO's 4.1-inch 16% pad.</strong> Both give 0.6 to 6.2 mph, a 16%
            maximum in three manual steps, 8.5-inch side handlebars, front wheels and the YPOOFIT app, and their Amazon
            product details give the same 43.5 by 21-inch footprint. They differ in small ways: 2/12/16% against
            0/12/16%, 300 against 330 lb, a 3.9 against a 4.1-inch fold, and 37.5 against 37.4-inch belts. On
            26 November 2026 the RHYTHM FUN listing was $118.99 and the YPOO one $139.99.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical point is the one our RHYTHM FUN guide made: before you buy either brand, search for the other
            and compare the photos, model code, belt, incline steps and capacity. If they match, buy the cheaper one from
            the seller whose returns you prefer. If they differ on capacity or belt length, buy on those figures, not on
            the logo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>YPOO Warranty, Returns and Who Sells It</h2>
<p>
            Every YPOO listing on this page gave "1 Year" (or "1-Year", or "1 Year Manufacturer") as its warranty in its
            Amazon product details on 26 November 2026. YPOO's store has its own one-year warranty, called YPOO Care,
            with conditions worth reading before you choose where to buy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What YPOO Care covers</h3>
          <p>
            YPOO's guarantee page says the product is "free from defects in material and workmanship for one (1) year
            from the date of purchase, provided it is used in a home environment". It excludes abuse, accidental damage,
            unauthorized repairs, negligence, misuse, modifications and commercial use, and it is non-transferable. The
            important line for Amazon shoppers is that YPOO Care "applies only to orders placed through ypoofitness.com".
            So on an Amazon purchase you rely on the one-year warranty stated in the listing, through the seller in the
            buy box. Keep the order number and write to YPOO's support email with photos if a fault appears. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how one year compares with what other brands offer.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns on YPOO's own store</h3>
          <p>
            The store's refund policy gives 30 days, for unused items in their <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a>, with no restocking
            fee. If the return is your choice, you pay return shipping, and that cost is deducted from the refund; if the
            item arrived damaged or wrong, you do not. Two lines need care. The policy says "only regular priced items may
            be refunded, unfortunately sale items cannot be refunded", which matters on Black Friday, and transit damage
            has to be reported within three business days of delivery, with photos.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who sells YPOO on Amazon</h3>
          <p>
            None of the YPOO listings we checked was sold by Amazon.com. Most were sold by YPOO-US, the brand's own
            storefront; the 4.1-inch 16% pad and both ultra-slim listings were sold by YIFIT FITNESS. That is normal for
            a brand like this, but it means the return terms are the seller's, inside Amazon's rules, rather than Amazon's
            own. Check the "Ships from" and "Sold by" lines and the seller's return terms before you order, and keep the
            box until the return window has closed: a 40 to 75 lb machine is very hard to send back without it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Store or Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            On 26 November 2026 Amazon was far cheaper. YPOO's store listed the M5038 ultra-slim pad at $209.99 against
            $149.99 with Prime on Amazon, and the V8 under the M4638 code at $441.99 against $199.99. The store's own
            YPOO Care warranty is also only one year, the same term the Amazon listings state, so the extra cost buys
            little except a direct relationship with the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before You Buy a YPOO Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            Because YPOO listings look alike and several contradict themselves, a few minutes on the listing page saves a
            return later. These are the checks worth making, in order.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The incline in the bullets, not the title.</strong> Two listings claim 13 or 16% in the title and
            8% in the bullets. If they disagree, assume the lower figure. Our guide to the <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a> explains how much incline is useful for walking.</li>
            <li><strong>The model code.</strong> Scroll to the product details and note it (T5, T7, M4138F, M5038 and so
            on). Two listings with the same photo can differ in speed and capacity, and the same code can appear twice at
            different prices.</li>
            <li><strong>The capacity.</strong> YPOO listings give 300, 330 or 350 lb, and one gives 350 and 400 lb in the
            same listing. Buy on the lowest figure stated and leave a margin; our <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity guide</a> explains why.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt length against your stride.</strong> Every YPOO belt figure is 36.6 to 39.5 inches. That is
            enough for walking for most adults; taller walkers and anyone who wants to jog should compare the longer belts
            in our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills
            under $500</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Whether the price needs Prime.</strong> Four YPOO deals on 26 November 2026 were Prime-exclusive.
            Without Prime, the ultra-slim pad was $199.99 or $239.99, not $149.99.</li>
            <li><strong>The seller line.</strong> YPOO-US or YIFIT FITNESS, not Amazon.com. Read that seller's return
            terms, and note that YPOO's own store warranty does not cover Amazon orders.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            One practical point on manual incline: on a YPOO pad you change it by lifting the frame and moving a spacer or
            lever, with the belt stopped. That is fine if you pick one setting for a session. If you want to change
            incline as you walk, you need a pad with <a href="/best-auto-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>automatic incline</a>, such as the MERACH alternative below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who a YPOO Treadmill Suits, and Who It Does Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A YPOO suits a walker on a tight budget who wants handles to hold and a fixed incline to climb. It does
            not suit a runner, a tall jogger or anyone who wants to change incline mid-walk.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> a home or desk walker who wants side handlebars and a steep manual incline for
            about $115 to $145. The 15% pad with handle bar fits this best.</li>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> users between 300 and 350 lb who want handles, on the T7, the highest-rated YPOO
            pad with handlebars.</li>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> an under-desk walker short of storage space, on the 3.5-inch M5038, as long as
            3.7 mph is enough.</li>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> a walker who wants a console, programs and the odd easy jog, on the M4638 folding
            treadmill, accepting its 37-inch belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not suit:</strong> runners. A belt of 36.6 to 39.5 inches and a ceiling of 8 mph are not
            running equipment; look for 10 mph or more and a belt of 55 to 60 inches. Our page on whether <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">you can run on a walking pad</a> explains the limits.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not suit:</strong> anyone who wants powered incline. Every YPOO on Amazon uses manual
            positions.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not suit:</strong> buyers who want long cover. One year is short; several rival brands give
            longer cover.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a household where two people of different sizes will share the machine, plan for the heavier user and
            the longer stride. That usually narrows the YPOO choice to the 350 lb models, or out of YPOO altogether.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>YPOO Alternatives: Bigger Brands and Powered Incline</h2>
<p>
            The two alternatives in the cards cover the two reasons YPOO shoppers end up looking elsewhere. If you would
            rather buy from Amazon itself and a brand with a longer US record, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro cost about $177.64 on
            26 November 2026, sold by Amazon.com, with a safety handle, a 12% manual incline, a running mode to 6.2 mph and
            a 12-month warranty. Its listing does not state a capacity or belt size, so check both. Our <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UREVO treadmills</a> guide covers the rest of
            that range.
          </p>
          <p>
            If manual incline is the sticking point, the MERACH W50 Lite TrekPad cost $209.99 on the same day, with a
            10-level automatic incline to 10% that you change by remote, a 350 lb rating and a 3.0 HP brushless motor. It
            has no side handlebars and its bullets give no speed range. Our <a href="/merach-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Merach treadmills</a> guide explains the
            brand's other pads and its warranty.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you need a real folding treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            YPOO's only folding treadmill on Amazon has a 37-inch belt. If you want to jog properly, a folding treadmill
            with a longer deck and a published continuous motor rating is a better buy, even at two or three times the
            price. Our guide to the <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a> compares
            them on belt, speed, capacity and folded size. For the closest look-alike brand, read our <a href="/rhythm-fun-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">RHYTHM FUN guide</a>, and for another
            budget brand read the same way, our guide to <a href="/serenelife-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SereneLife treadmills</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Black Friday</a> falls on 27 November 2026, and YPOO listings already showed cuts of $21 to $70 against their list
            or typical prices on 26 November. A cut against a list price is not the same as a good price, and four of
            the best deals needed Prime. Compare the model code and the price on the day rather than the percentage off,
            and remember that YPOO's own store does not refund sale items.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes People Make Buying a YPOO Walking Pad</h2>
          <p>A YPOO can be a sensible cheap incline walker. These are the mistakes that turn it into a return.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Believing the incline in the title</h3>
              <p>Two YPOO listings claim 13 or 16% in the title and 8% in the bullets. Read the bullets and the model code, assume the lower figure, and do not pay extra for a bigger number.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Counting on a Prime-only price</h3>
              <p>Four YPOO deals on 26 November 2026 were Prime-exclusive, and the same listings cost $50 to $96 more without Prime. Check the price in your basket, not on the search page.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming YPOO’s store warranty covers Amazon orders</h3>
              <p>YPOO Care applies only to orders placed on ypoofitness.com. On Amazon you rely on the one-year warranty in the listing and on the seller, so keep the order number and the box.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying one to run on</h3>
              <p>Every YPOO belt is about 37 to 39.5 inches long and the fastest model tops out at 8 mph. If you plan to run, budget for a treadmill with a 55 to 60-inch belt and 10 mph or more.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>Our verdict on the brand</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">YPOO is a real budget brand that makes incline walking pads, not running
          treadmills.</strong> The 15% pad with handle bar at $116.84 is the one to buy on price, the T7 at $142.49 adds a
          350 lb rating and 7.5 mph, the ultra-slim M5038 at $149.99 with Prime is the under-desk pick, and the M4638 at
          $199.99 with Prime is the only YPOO with a console (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026). Every model has a short belt, a manual
          incline and a one-year warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the bullets rather than the title, check the model code, the lowest capacity and whether the price needs
          Prime, and keep the box. Compare any YPOO with its RHYTHM FUN look-alike before you buy. If you want to run, or
          to change incline as you walk, compare <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmills under $500</a> or our <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">incline walking pads</a> before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

