        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is YPOO a good treadmill brand?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a legitimate budget brand with a US store, a US phone line and spare parts for sale. Its walking pads are fine for incline walking with handles, but every model has a belt of about 37 to 39.5 inches, a top speed of 8 mph or less, a manual incline and a one-year warranty, so they are not running treadmills.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who makes YPOO treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              YPOO’s US store says the brand started in 2010. A factory site under the same name, ypoo.com, describes YPOO as a Chinese manufacturer of treadmills and other equipment in Wuyi County, Jinhua, Zhejiang, that also builds for other brands. We could not confirm the two are the same legal company.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is YPOO the same as RHYTHM FUN?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They are clearly linked: RHYTHM FUN uses YPOO’s YPOOFIT app and calls its S8 a “YPOO treadmill”, and YPOO’s shipping guide names Rhythm Fun. Some models look like the same design, but their Amazon listings differ on model codes, belts or capacity, so compare the figures before assuming two are identical.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty comes with a YPOO walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Every Amazon listing we checked gives one year. YPOO’s own YPOO Care warranty is also one year, for home use, but it applies only to orders placed on ypoofitness.com, so on an Amazon order the listing’s warranty and the seller are what you rely on.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much weight can a YPOO walking pad hold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              YPOO listings give 300, 330 or 350 lb depending on the model. The T7 and the ultra-slim M5038 state 350 lb. One listing states both 350 and 400 lb, so use the lower figure for that one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the YPOO incline automatic?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Every YPOO on Amazon on 26 November 2026 used a manual incline, set by hand at two or three positions with the belt stopped. If you want to change incline while walking, look at an automatic-incline pad such as the MERACH W50 Lite.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What app does YPOO use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              YPOO pads use the YPOOFIT app, spelled Ypoofit or YPOO FIT in some listings, for workouts, speed control and tracking. The same app is used by RHYTHM FUN’s pads.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
